body:
I was about to file a new issue for PathFollow3D not handling all forms of valid bezier curves correctly but it may be related to this issue.

See this example of a path that clearly shows the problem:

This uses 5 points:
```
pos: < 0, 0, 1>                out: < 1, 0, 0>
pos: < 1, 0, 0> in: < 0, 0, 0> out: < 0, 0, 0>
pos: < 0, 0,-1> in: < 1, 0, 0> out: <-1, 0, 0>
pos: <-1, 0, 0> in: < 0, 0, 0> out: < 0, 0, 0>
pos: < 0, 0, 1> in: <-1, 0, 0>
```

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8c503352-078d-4743-bd35-015cbc70c832)

This seems to be caused by some oversights in the curve baking calculations where the tangent is calculated using the derivative of the curve.  Remember that the derivative encodes the rate of change of the values of the curve which can go to 0 in several cases:

- When the curve is a quadratic curve (one of the 2 control points is collinear with the other three control points)
- When the curve is linear (the curve is just the end points so a line)
- When the curve is a point (all control points are collinear)

Looking at the code there is an attempt to correct for some of these edge cases but it actually produces the wrong output.  The code currently does this:

```
Vector3 Curve3D::_calculate_tangent(const Vector3 &p_begin, const Vector3 &p_control_1, const Vector3 &p_control_2, const Vector3 &p_end, const real_t p_t) {
	// Handle corner cases.
	if (Math::is_zero_approx(p_t - 0.0f) && p_control_1.is_equal_approx(p_begin)) {
		return (p_end - p_begin).normalized();
	}

	if (Math::is_zero_approx(p_t - 1.0f) && p_control_2.is_equal_approx(p_end)) {
		return (p_end - p_begin).normalized();
	}

	return p_begin.bezier_derivative(p_control_1, p_control_2, p_end, p_t).normalized();
}
```

That is the code right now assumes if either control point is collinear with the endpoints of the curve that the curve is linear; however, if the two control points are not also both collinear the curve is actually quadratic and the tangent should use the quadratic formula instead.

As the singularity only exists at the end points it might be enough to extend the handling of the corner cases like this:

```
Vector3 Curve3D::_calculate_tangent(const Vector3 &p_begin, const Vector3 &p_control_1, const Vector3 &p_control_2, const Vector3 &p_end, const real_t p_t) {
	// Handle corner cases.
	if (Math::is_zero_approx(p_t - 0.0f)) {
		if (p_begin.is_equal_approx(p_control_1)) {
			if (p_begin.is_equal_approx(p_control_2)) {
				return (p_end - p_begin).normalized(); // linear curve
			}
			return (p_control_2 - p_begin).normalized(); // quadratic curve
		}
	} else if (Math::is_zero_approx(p_t - 1.0f)) {
		if (p_end.is_equal_approx(p_control_2)) {
			if (p_end.is_equal_approx(p_control_1)) {
				return (p_end - p_begin).normalized(); // linear curve
			}
			return (p_end - p_control_1).normalized(); // quadratic curve
		}
	}

	return p_begin.bezier_derivative(p_control_1, p_control_2, p_end, p_t).normalized();
}
```

This would fix the problems where the standard cubic derivative is 0 but the quadratic is not.  Note that this does not fix the case where all four points are collinear which is still a valid bezier curve. There isn't any reasonable tangent that can be returned from this function though and can only be handled at the higher level of a path with multiple curve segments.

Fundamentally these edge cases may need to be handled in the `_bake`  function itself though as baking a curve for path following doesn't actually care about the true derivative as the path is a function of distance and not time.

